# The OS half of Umbra's `kernel` capability.
#
# On Android only init may read or write the security-sensitive sysctls; no app
# and no other service can, whatever it is signed with. So the app asks through a
# property and init does the work here.
#
#   persist.umbra.kernel.harden=1             harden now
#   persist.umbra.kernel.harden=0             stop holding it
#   persist.umbra.kernel.prior_perf_harden    what perf hardening was before
#
# What "harden" does:
#   - re-asserts the three values Android's own init.rc sets at every boot
#     (hidden kernel pointers, full ASLR, no setuid core dumps), in case a
#     profiling tool lowered one since. They are the OS's normal values, so
#     there is nothing to put back afterwards.
#   - turns on Android's perf-event lockdown. security.perf_harden is the OS's
#     own switch for this; init-perfetto.rc reacts by writing
#     kernel.perf_event_paranoid=3. That sysctl is the one of the set an app CAN
#     read, so the app reads it back as proof that this action ran.
#
# Deliberately not here: dmesg and ptrace are already denied to every app by
# SELinux on Android, and reverse-path filtering must stay off because Android's
# multi-network routing depends on it.

# Lets the app tell an OS that ships this file from one that does not.
on init
    setprop umbra.kernel.hook 1

on property:persist.umbra.kernel.harden=1
    write /proc/sys/kernel/kptr_restrict 2
    write /proc/sys/kernel/randomize_va_space 2
    write /proc/sys/fs/suid_dumpable 0
    setprop security.perf_harden 1

# Put perf hardening back to what the app recorded before it hardened. If it
# recorded nothing, stay hardened, which is also Android's default.
on property:persist.umbra.kernel.harden=0
    setprop security.perf_harden ${persist.umbra.kernel.prior_perf_harden:-1}
